1. INTRODUCTION 

Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) has emerged as one of the major challenges affecting the effective treatment of bacterial infections. The continued and inappropriate use of antibiotics has contributed to the selection and dissemination of resistant microorganisms, reducing the effectiveness of several commonly used antimicrobial agents. The development of multidrug-resistant bacteria is particularly concerning because resistance to multiple antibiotic classes can substantially restrict available therapeutic options. Consequently, the identification of new antibacterial targets and the development of alternative strategies for overcoming bacterial resistance have become important areas of biomedical research. 

Bacteria employ several mechanisms to survive exposure to antimicrobial compounds. These include enzymatic inactivation of antibiotics, modification of antibiotic targets, changes in membrane permeability, alteration of metabolic pathways, and active removal of toxic compounds from the cell. Among these mechanisms, active drug efflux is particularly important because efflux systems can recognize and transport chemically diverse compounds out of bacterial cells, thereby reducing their intracellular concentrations. Multidrug efflux pumps are therefore considered important contributors to bacterial multidrug resistance. 

The resistance-nodulation-division (RND) family represents one of the major groups of multidrug efflux systems found in Gram-negative bacteria. These transporters use the proton motive force across the bacterial inner membrane to drive the export of substrates. In Pseudomonas aeruginosa, the MexAB-OprM efflux system is one of the best-characterized RND systems and contributes to resistance against a broad range of antimicrobial compounds. MexB functions as the inner-membrane transporter component of this system and plays a central role in substrate recognition and transport. 

Structural studies of RND transporters have shown that these proteins do not function as static structures. Instead, the three protomers of the transporter can adopt different conformations during the transport cycle. The structures have been described as Access, Binding, and Extrusion states, corresponding to different stages of substrate recognition, binding, and export. This asymmetric arrangement provides a structural explanation for the functional rotation of the transporter and demonstrates why analysis of a single protein conformation may not fully describe the behaviour of an RND efflux pump (Murakami et al., 2006; Seeger et al., 2006). 

The MexB transporter contains an extensive periplasmic porter domain that forms the principal region involved in recognition and transport of substrates. Within this region, distinct binding environments have been identified, including regions capable of accommodating structurally diverse compounds. The internal architecture of the porter domain is therefore an important consideration when investigating possible strategies for interfering with substrate recognition or transport (Nakashima et al., 2013). 

The deep binding region is of particular interest because interactions within this internal cavity may influence the recognition and movement of substrates through the transporter. Aromatic and hydrophobic residues present within the cavity can contribute to ligand stabilization through hydrophobic and π-related interactions, while other residues may participate in polar or electrostatic interactions. Characterizing these residues and their spatial arrangement can therefore provide useful information for computational screening of compounds that may interact with the transporter. 

Traditional experimental discovery of efflux-pump inhibitors can be resource-intensive because candidate compounds must be identified, synthesized or obtained, tested for activity, and subsequently characterized. Computational approaches can reduce the initial search space by allowing large numbers of compounds to be evaluated before experimental testing. Molecular docking, virtual screening, structural comparison, and machine-learning-based binding-site prediction are among the approaches that can be integrated for this purpose. 

Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine-learning approaches have increasingly expanded the capabilities of computational drug discovery. These methods can analyse large chemical and biological datasets, identify patterns associated with molecular activity, predict protein structures and binding sites, and assist in prioritizing candidate compounds. AI-based approaches have been applied to target identification, virtual screening, molecular property prediction, drug repurposing, antimicrobial discovery, and resistance prediction. 

The increasing availability of experimentally determined and computationally predicted protein structures has further strengthened structure-based drug discovery. Protein structural information can be used to identify potential ligand-binding cavities and characterize the amino-acid environment surrounding these regions. Machine-learning-based pocket prediction methods can assist in locating potential binding sites that may not be readily identified through visual inspection alone. P2Rank is one such approach that predicts ligand-binding pockets from protein structural information and assigns scores to candidate sites (Krivák and Hoksza, 2018). 

Molecular docking can subsequently be used to investigate how candidate compounds may interact with a selected binding pocket. Docking methods generate possible ligand orientations within a protein cavity and provide scoring functions that can be used to prioritize predicted binding modes. AutoDock Vina is a widely used docking program that estimates binding affinity while exploring possible ligand conformations and orientations within a defined search region (Eberhardt et al., 2021). 

The combination of structural analysis, binding-site prediction, molecular docking, and residue-level interaction analysis therefore provides a systematic approach for investigating potential ligands against structurally defined protein targets. Such workflows do not establish biological activity directly, but they can identify compounds and interaction patterns that warrant further experimental investigation. 

In the present study, MexB from Pseudomonas aeruginosa was investigated as a structurally defined multidrug efflux-pump target. The biological assembly of the MexB structure was analysed to distinguish the three functional conformational states represented by the Access, Binding, and Extrusion protomers. Structural alignment of the transmembrane regions was used to compare the conformations while minimizing the influence of overall membrane-domain orientation. Changes in the porter domain were subsequently examined to characterize conformational differences associated with the transport cycle. 

The Binding-state structure was selected for detailed investigation of the Deep Binding Pocket (DBP). P2Rank was used to identify potential ligand-binding regions, after which the functionally relevant pocket within the porter domain was selected for computational screening. The residues defining the selected pocket were subsequently examined to establish a core interaction set for evaluating ligand–protein contacts. 

A collection of candidate compounds was then subjected to molecular docking against the selected DBP using AutoDock Vina. Docking scores and predicted interactions with the predefined core residues were used to prioritize compounds. Experimentally characterized reference ligands were also examined computationally to determine whether the selected binding region could reproduce interaction patterns associated with known MexB-related compounds. 

The overall objective of this study was therefore to establish a structure-based computational workflow for characterizing the MexB Deep Binding Pocket and prioritizing potential ligands for further investigation. By integrating conformational analysis, machine-learning-based pocket prediction, molecular docking, and residue-level interaction assessment, the study provides a computational framework for exploring ligand recognition within an important bacterial multidrug efflux transporter. 

 
